your minister.

54
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:32,060
Oh, right, because when a multimillion-dollar flagship program just crashes due to siloed data, the minister is the one whose agenda fails.

55
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:39,060
Yes. Their cross ministry programs fail to land because the underlying systems simply refuse to share data.

56
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,580
Yeah. They cannot deliver on their biggest political promises.

57
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:49,000
So the root cause of all this friction is simply that there is no shared plan of what the digital systems actually are and, you know, who owns them.

58
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:55,220
Exactly. And the solution is a national enterprise architecture, because you cannot rewire a building you have never mapped.

59
00:03:55,360 --> 00:04:00,920
That mapping is exactly what a national EA does, and the good news is you don't have to invent this map from scratch.

60
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:07,840
Right. There are established frameworks out there, like the European Interoperability Framework and P-A-E-R-A or PAERA.

61
00:04:08,109 --> 00:04:10,660
And just to be clear on how those frameworks actually work,

62
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:13,500
they essentially provide a standardized checklist, right?

63
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:22,320
Mm-hmm. They dictate exactly how data must be formatted and shared so that any new system you purchase is mathematically guaranteed to plug into the old ones.

64
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:28,680
Exactly. It sets the technical rules of the road, and following those frameworks is, um, surprisingly efficient.

65
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:34,380
Is it? Yeah. It takes just six months to get to a first roadmap requiring only four key sign-offs.

66
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:42,980
